  <h1>They Walk Among Us</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">April 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 2</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/they-walk-among-us.html?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><span class="page-next disabled">Next &#8594;</span></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>But she could teach them while they were here. She could stand at the front of the room and open the map and say <em>today we're going to learn about rivers</em> and mean it, in every sense of the word, because a river was a thing that kept moving even when parts of it slowed or stopped, even when the water at the bottom was dark and still.</p>
<p>Earl had worked for the city for twenty years before he became a crossing guard, and the two jobs were not as different as they might have seemed. In both, he was a man standing in a place where people needed to get somewhere else, helping them cross. The only difference was what he was helping them cross, and what waited on the other side.</p>
<p>The intersection of Maple and 5th was not a bad intersection. It was busy in the mornings and moderately busy in the afternoons and quiet the rest of the time. There was a elementary school two blocks east, and the children who walked to and from it used this crossing because it had the light, because it had the crosswalk, because Earl was there every morning at 7:40 and every afternoon at 3:05, standing in the road with his hand up, his vest bright in the early light or the late light, his face the first adult face some of these children would see each day.</p>
<p>He knew their names. Not all of them — there were too many, and they changed every year as the school population shifted — but enough of them. The girl with the red backpack who was afraid of the big trucks. The twin boys who were never quite in step with each other no matter how hard they tried. The small quiet child who always, without fail, said <em>good morning Mr. Earl</em> and always, without fail, did not smile, not because he was unhappy but because he was serious, because the business of crossing the street was serious business to a child who took it literally.</p>
<p>Earl had a photograph in his nightstand drawer. He did not look at it often. He did not need to. He had looked at it enough in the early years that it had burned itself into the place behind his eyes where things lived without being seen. In the photograph, he was younger and in uniform and standing in front of a building that no longer existed, and beside him stood a woman named Delia who had been his partner for eleven years, and beside her stood the work — the real work, the work he could not describe, the work he had left the job rather than continue.</p>
<p>He did not think of it as running away. He had thought about that carefully, in the months after he left, turning the question over like a stone in his hand. He had not run. He had walked, deliberately, away — away from the building, away from the uniform, away from the particular weight that had settled into his shoulders and his back and the back of his eyes and had refused, absolutely, to be set down. He had left because staying would have meant becoming something he could not become, something that had no name except the absence of the man he wanted to continue to be.</p>
<p>The crossing guard job was not a retirement. It was a replacement — not of income, but of purpose. It was life after aftermath. It was morning instead of the thing that comes after morning. It was children who were going somewhere, arriving somewhere, becoming someone — as opposed to the other kind of arrival, the kind he had witnessed too many times, the kind that was an ending rather than a beginning.</p>
<p>He did not explain this to anyone. There was no one to explain it to. His ex-wife lived in another state. His sister called on holidays. The other crossing guards were nice people who talked about their knees and the weather, and Earl talked about those things too, because that was what you talked about when you were a crossing guard, and because the conversation was pleasant in the way that surface things are pleasant when you have learned to let surface things be surface things and not look beneath them.</p>
<p>Some mornings, when the light was good and the children were filing past in their small determined way, Earl felt something move through him that was not happiness but was adjacent to it, in the next room, within earshot. It was enough. It was more than enough. It was a kind of proof that the part of him he had thought might be gone for good was still there, still functioning, still capable of being moved by a child's red backpack or a <em>good morning</em> from a boy who took his job seriously.</p>
<p>Raymond, Maria, and Earl never met. They rode no buses together. They attended no meetings. They passed each other on streets and sidewalks without knowing that the other existed, without knowing that the woman walking briskly with her coffee was a woman who had buried a son, that the man behind the wheel of the stopped bus was a man who had buried a war, that the crossing guard at the corner was a man who had buried a version of himself so complete it was as though it had never been.</p>
<p>This is how it works. This is how it has always worked.</p>
<p>The city held them the way the city holds everyone — indifferently, steadily, without asking what anyone was carrying. They went to work. They came home. They ate dinner. They sat in rooms with the weight of their own particular burdens, which they bore without display and without announcement, because that is what people do when the burden is too large to share — they carry it themselves, and they go on, and the going on is the thing that looks, from the outside, like ordinary life.</p>
<p>Raymond finished his last run at 4:47 in the afternoon, the same time every day. He pulled the bus into the depot, checked the fluid levels, noted a small rattle in the engine that he would mention to the mechanic, and walked to his car. Denise had left a note on the kitchen table: <em>Leftovers in the blue container. Heating instructions: 3 minutes, no more.</em> He read it twice, the way he always did, because the notes were small acts of continuity, proof that someone knew he was coming home.</p>
<p>Maria stayed an hour past the final bell, as she always did — grading, organizing, leaving a sticky note on the desk of the student who had seemed off that day, a note that said only <em>You did good work today</em> because sometimes that was the only true thing you could say. Then she drove home along the river road, which was not the most direct route but was the one she preferred, because the water was there and the sky was often wide and open and the drive gave her time to become the person who would walk through her own front door.</p>
<p>Earl finished his shift and walked to the diner on Garrison where he drank coffee and read the paper and did not think about the photograph. On Thursdays, the waitress named Pauline brought him a slice of pie without him ordering it, because after eleven years she knew what Thursdays were. He always thanked her. He always meant it.</p>
<p>These are the hours that make a life. Not the large hours. Not the dramatic ones. The small ones, the ordinary ones, the hours where you are simply present in your own existence, doing the next thing, and the next thing, and the next thing, because that is what is required, because that is what is here, because the morning will come again and you will meet it, and you will raise your hand, and you will say <em>good morning</em>, and you will mean it.</p>
<p>The world is full of people like this. They walk among us. They have always walked among us.</p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
